"Stand on the west coast of Taiwan on a clear morning and look east. A wall of mountains fills the sky. The highest of them, Yushan, is 3,952 meters tall, and 268 peaks on this small island are higher than 3,000 meters. Yet a few million years ago, there was no island here at all. There was only the sea.": "c418a10a0974",
"Stand on the west coast of Taiwan on a clear morning and look east. A wall of mountains fills the sky. The highest of them, Yushan, is 3,952 meters tall, and 268 peaks on this small island are higher than 3,000 meters. Yet a few million years ago, there was no island here at all. There was only the sea. The mountains came from a slow collision. In Lesson 2 you saw that the Philippine Sea Plate pushes toward the northwest, about 7 centimeters a year. In front of it lay the edge of the continent, covered with thick layers of mud and sand under the water. As the plate pushed in, those layers had nowhere to go. So they went up. Think of pushing a rug across the floor against a wall: the rug wrinkles and rises into folds. In the same way, the layers of the seabed were squeezed, folded, and piled on top of one another until they rose out of the sea. That pile is the island of Taiwan, and it formed only about 4 to 5 million years ago. The pushing has not stopped. The piled-up rock is still rising, by about 5 millimeters a year. That sounds like nothing. But in a million years, 5 millimeters a year adds up to 5 kilometers. If that were the whole story, Taiwan's mountains would be taller than any on Earth. They are not, because something is taking them apart. Rain, rivers, typhoons, and landslides break the rock and carry it downhill as gravel, sand, and mud. The steeper the slope, the faster the water runs and the more it can carry. In the lowlands the rivers slow down and drop their sand, and the plains where most people live are built from pieces of the mountains. So a mountain is not a thing that sits still. It is more like a fountain: water is always rising and always falling, and the shape stays. Rock is pushed up from below and carried away from the top, year after year. The mountains you see are what is left over, for now, in a contest that is still going on.": "e784968784f8",
